                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "(L)-Proline's production and use in parenteral nutrition, as a dietary supplement, and as a starting material for pharmaceutical manufacture may result in its release to the environment through various waste streams. (L)-Proline is present in many plants and is a non-essential amino acid for human development. If released to air, an estimated vapor pressure of 3.0X10-8 mm Hg at 25 °C indicates (L)-proline will exist solely in the particulate phase in the atmosphere. Particulate-phase (L)-proline will be removed from the atmosphere by wet and dry deposition. (L)-Proline does not contain chromophores that absorb at wavelengths >290 nm and, therefore, is not expected to be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight. If released to soil, (L)-proline is expected to have very high mobility based upon an estimated Koc of 3.4. The pKa values of (L)-proline are 1.99 and 10.60, indicating that this compound will exist as a zwitterion in the environment and zwitterions generally adsorb more strongly to soils containing organic carbon and clay than their neutral counterparts. Volatilization from moist soil is not expected because the compound exists as an ion and ions do not volatilize.(L)-Proline is not expected to volatilize from dry soil surfaces based upon its vapor pressure. Using aquatic screening tests, 100% biodegradation of (L)-proline was reported, suggesting that biodegradation is an important environmental fate process in soil and water. If released into water, (L)-proline is not expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the estimated Koc. The pKa values indicate (L)-proline will exist as a zwitterion at pH values of 5 to 9 and, therefore, volatilization from water surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process. An estimated BCF of 3 suggests the potential for bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is low. Hydrolysis is not expected to be an important environmental fate process since this compound 